From: guillermo <guillermo.karsz@solid-run.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 14:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] hummingboard-iiot: boot light on the RGB LED and power LED from the device tree

The LP5562 drives the RGB LED (D7) and the green LED D8 behind the PWR label of
the enclosure. Use the kernel's pattern trigger (CONFIG_LEDS_TRIGGER_PATTERN) so
that, from driver probe, the RGB LED fades red -> green -> blue -> red over four
seconds and then settles on a tuned white (255/160/90, full drive looks blue on
this LED), held until the next boot. D8 is switched on as the power indicator.
The labels keep the LED names R, G, B and D8 used by the production tests; they
are required for the driver to register the LEDs with their DT node (see the
leds-lp55xx patch).

Signed-off-by: guillermo <guillermo.karsz@solid-run.com>
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/hailo/hummingboard-iiot.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/hailo/hummingboard-iiot.dtsi
@@ -126,6 +126,10 @@
 
         chan@0 {
             chan-name = "R";
+            label = "R";
+            /* boot light, see hbiiot RGB pattern: red, green, blue, red, then white held */
+            linux,default-trigger = "pattern";
+            led-pattern = <255 1000 0 1000 0 1000 255 1000 255 4294967295>;
             led-cur = /bits/ 8 <0x20>;
             max-cur = /bits/ 8 <0x60>;
             reg = <0>;
@@ -134,6 +138,9 @@
 
         chan@1 {
             chan-name = "G";
+            label = "G";
+            linux,default-trigger = "pattern";
+            led-pattern = <0 1000 255 1000 0 1000 0 1000 160 4294967295>;
             led-cur = /bits/ 8 <0x20>;
             max-cur = /bits/ 8 <0x60>;
             reg = <1>;
@@ -142,6 +149,9 @@
 
         chan@2 {
             chan-name = "B";
+            label = "B";
+            linux,default-trigger = "pattern";
+            led-pattern = <0 1000 0 1000 255 1000 0 1000 90 4294967295>;
             led-cur = /bits/ 8 <0x20>;
             max-cur = /bits/ 8 <0x60>;
             reg = <2>;
@@ -150,6 +160,9 @@
 
         chan@3 {
             chan-name = "D8";
+            label = "D8";
+            /* power indicator (PWR on the enclosure): on from driver probe */
+            linux,default-trigger = "default-on";
             led-cur = /bits/ 8 <0x20>;
             max-cur = /bits/ 8 <0x60>;
             reg = <3>;
